TypeScript is a programming language that was developed by Microsoft in 2011 as an alternative to JavaScript. It is designed to provide a more concise, expressive, and safer way of writing code compared to traditional JavaScript.

Duck typing is a feature of TypeScript that allows developers to specify the type of an object at compile-time rather than runtime. This means that if you write code like this:

```typescript
class Animal {
  name: string;
  age: number;

  constructor(name: string, age: number) {
    this.name = name;
    this.age = age;
  }
}
```

The compiler will automatically infer the correct type for `this` based on the inferred types of `name` and `age`. For example, it might infer that `Animal` is a class with properties `name` and `age`, so the compiler can safely use them without worrying about their actual values.

This feature makes TypeScript much more flexible and powerful than traditional JavaScript, allowing developers to write more readable and maintainable code. However, it also requires careful attention to detail when using duck typing, as not all objects have the same type constraints. 

Overall, TypeScript provides a powerful tool for writing high-quality, safe, and efficient code, while also being more flexible and customizable than traditional JavaScript.

